  "worlds": [
    {
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "start_sec": 0.0,
      "end_sec": 208.0,
      "base_prompt": "Compose ONE single continuous scene that fills the whole frame: a single moment, seen once, from one camera. Never a grid, never panels, never a storyboard sheet, never borders, gutters, insets, numbered boxes or caption strips.  Visual treatment: hand-drawn editorial history illustration on warm parchment. Minimalist human figures with round white heads, small simple black facial features, thin expressive limbs, and simple period-appropriate clothing. Identity is carried by clothing colour, silhouette, headwear and props — never by facial detail. Visible ink contour lines, soft watercolour and gouache shading, muted ochre, rust, umber, sage and desaturated teal. Readable silhouettes, layered foreground, middle ground and background, one unmistakable story action per frame, and clear negative space in the lower third for captions. Reuse the same clothing colours, props and location design whenever they recur. Composition must read instantly at phone size. No text, letters, numbers, labels, arrows, UI, watermark, or accidental writing; the renderer adds all typography and diagram overlays. Recurring identities: Broussard in a tan suit and bow tie; the purple water hyacinth as the small visual villain; the grey hippo as the oversized solution. Keep clothing colour, silhouette and props stable across every appearance.",
      "on_screen_label": ""
    }
  ],
  "narration": [
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_001",
      "start_sec": 0,
      "end_sec": 6,
      "narration": "America once considered fighting a weed with hippopotamuses. The official plan had three steps.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_002",
      "start_sec": 6,
      "end_sec": 15,
      "narration": "Step one: ship hippos to Louisiana. Step two: let them eat the weed. Step three: eat the hippos. This was not a joke.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_003",
      "start_sec": 15,
      "end_sec": 22,
      "narration": "It reached a congressional hearing. And somehow, the hippos were only the second-worst invasive species in the story.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_004",
      "start_sec": 22,
      "end_sec": 32,
      "narration": "The first arrived in New Orleans in 1884. It was a pretty Amazonian plant called water hyacinth, displayed at an exposition and handed out like a fashionable souvenir.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_005",
      "start_sec": 32,
      "end_sec": 40,
      "narration": "Gardeners put it in ponds. When it multiplied, they tossed the extras into canals. The pretty souvenir then began eating the map.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_006",
      "start_sec": 40,
      "end_sec": 50,
      "narration": "Within two decades, thick mats clogged Southern waterways, blocked boats, damaged trade, and gave mosquitoes a floating kingdom. Workers cut it, dredged it, even burned it. It returned.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_007",
      "start_sec": 50,
      "end_sec": 60,
      "narration": "At the same time, meat prices were rising. Shoppers blamed butchers. Butchers blamed packers. Packers blamed cattle and grain. America wanted cheaper meat and fewer weeds.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_008",
      "start_sec": 60,
      "end_sec": 68,
      "narration": "Louisiana Representative Robert Broussard looked at both crises and saw one enormous, amphibious answer. What if the weed could become bacon?",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_009",
      "start_sec": 68,
      "end_sec": 77,
      "narration": "On March 24, 1910, he brought the idea before the House Committee on Agriculture: spend 250,000 dollars importing useful African animals, especially hippos.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_010",
      "start_sec": 77,
      "end_sec": 85,
      "narration": "Newspapers supplied the perfect brand: “lake cow bacon.” A government researcher said hippo meat tasted like pork and beef combined.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_011",
      "start_sec": 85,
      "end_sec": 95,
      "narration": "Broussard's witnesses included a celebrated frontier scout and a South African adventurer who later became a German spy. Apparently the hippo pitch needed both agriculture and espionage.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_012",
      "start_sec": 95,
      "end_sec": 105,
      "narration": "The committee asked sensible questions. Would hippos stay behind ordinary fences? Yes, the advocates said. Would they happily live on water hyacinth? Absolutely. What could go wrong?",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_013",
      "start_sec": 105,
      "end_sec": 114,
      "narration": "Congress did something unusually valuable. It paused. The committee shelved the bill. So let us take it off the shelf and run the experiment ourselves.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_014",
      "start_sec": 114,
      "end_sec": 127,
      "narration": "First problem: the menu. Hippos spend much of the day in water, but they mostly leave it at night to graze on land. Water hyacinth is about 95 percent water and offers little nutrition.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_015",
      "start_sec": 127,
      "end_sec": 136,
      "narration": "Our living weed machines might ignore the job they were imported to do—and start competing for pasture instead. The weed remains. The feed bill arrives.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_016",
      "start_sec": 136,
      "end_sec": 148,
      "narration": "Second problem: the output. Hippos return nutrients to water in industrial quantities. That can fuel algae, reduce water quality, and harm native fish. The cleanup animal can become a pollution machine.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_017",
      "start_sec": 148,
      "end_sec": 161,
      "narration": "Third problem: escape. Decades later, Colombia accidentally tested this with four hippos from Pablo Escobar's private zoo. Their descendants multiplied, spread through waterways, and became an ecological problem that is still difficult to control.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_018",
      "start_sec": 161,
      "end_sec": 171,
      "narration": "Now picture that experiment beginning in the American South in 1910: hippo fences, hippo patrol boats, hippo road signs, and a brand-new Department of Hippo Affairs.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_019",
      "start_sec": 171,
      "end_sec": 179,
      "narration": "America did not merely miss a new kind of bacon. It avoided turning a purple plant invasion into a two-ton mammal invasion.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_020",
      "start_sec": 179,
      "end_sec": 190,
      "narration": "The strangest part is that the first invader had arrived as a beautiful solution for gardens. Then experts proposed a second invader as the beautiful solution to the first.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_021",
      "start_sec": 190,
      "end_sec": 197,
      "narration": "The plan had three steps. It only forgot step four: what happens if the solution refuses the job?",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "scene_022",
      "start_sec": 197,
      "end_sec": 208,
      "narration": "So whenever one idea claims to solve two problems at once, ask what new problem just walked into the room—especially if it weighs two tons and can swim.",
      "world_id": "illustrated_history",
      "story_role": "beat",
      "claim_ids": []
    }
  ],
